# One-shot resumption nested inside Loom's own continuations
# (docs/oneshot-design.md 11.11): a control handler installed on a *virtual*
# thread, suspending and resuming while the thread it runs on is being
# unmounted and remounted underneath it.
#
# This is the shape production runs in. dawnop-site serves every request on
# `newVirtualThreadPerTaskExecutor`, so a `ctl` handler installed in a request
# handler puts a `jdk.internal.vm.Continuation` of ours *inside* the scheduler
# continuation Loom already has mounted. Loom says nested continuations are
# supported; nothing here exercised it, and the answer decides whether the
# effect system can be adopted by a server at all.
#
# The last case is the one that would have been the finding. A carrier is
# pinned when a frame that cannot be frozen is on the stack, and a pinned
# virtual thread does not unmount: it blocks its carrier instead. Nothing
# throws, nothing is printed, and the only symptom is that the pool stops
# making progress -- which is invisible unless the pool is small enough for
# one blocked carrier to be the whole of it. So the scheduler is pinned to a
# single carrier and the question is asked as a deadlock that resolves or does
# not: `parked` awaits a latch from inside a mounted control activation, and
# `other` can only reach `countDown` if `parked` really unmounted. A pinning
# runtime prints `awaited=false` after the two-second timeout, and prints it
# *before* the other thread's line rather than after.
#
# JVM-only, and not because of `ctl`: virtual threads are a JVM runtime fact,
# not a language feature, so the program is written with `use java` and the
# native backend refuses it by design (see run.sh's marker note). There is no
# native half of this question -- native's carriers are pthreads the runtime
# owns and Dawn cannot spell (docs/oneshot-design.md 11.11).
use std/io
use java "java.lang.System"
use java "java.lang.Thread"
use java "java.util.concurrent.CountDownLatch"
use java "java.util.concurrent.TimeUnit"

ctl effect Ask {
  fn ask(n: Int) -> Int
}

# The baseline: nothing about this handler knows what kind of thread it is on.
# Two raises, so a continuation is captured and resumed twice, all of it inside
# the scheduler continuation Loom mounted to run this body.
fn installed_on_a_virtual_thread() -> String = {
  with handle Ask { ask(n) resume k => k(n + 1) }
  "installed ${to_string(ask(1) + ask(10))}"
}

# The arm sleeps before it resumes. Our continuation is frozen for the whole
# sleep, so what unmounts is a virtual thread whose stack holds a *suspended*
# activation of ours -- the easy half of the nesting, and the half a
# `Thread.sleep` between suspension and resumption provokes for free.
fn suspended_across_a_sleep() -> String !io = {
  with handle Ask {
    ask(n) resume k => {
      Thread.sleep(20)
      k(n + 1)
    }
  }
  "slept ${to_string(ask(1) + ask(10))}"
}

# The hard half, in the order that makes it hard: unmount first, then yield.
# `Thread.sleep` inside the block freezes Loom's continuation *past* our
# mounted one and thaws it again; the `ask` after it then freezes our own
# frames, which have already been through one of Loom's freeze/thaw round
# trips. A mechanism that cached anything about where its frames were would
# answer wrong here and nowhere above.
fn yields_after_a_remount() -> String !io = {
  with handle Ask { ask(n) resume k => k(n + 1) }
  Thread.sleep(20)
  "remounted ${to_string(ask(1) + ask(10))}"
}

# Does a mounted control activation pin the carrier? The block suspends and
# resumes first, so what awaits the latch is the *resumed* remainder running
# inside `Continuation.run`, and the await is a park -- the operation a pinned
# virtual thread has to serve on its carrier instead of unmounting for.
#
# The timeout is the oracle's other half. Without it a pinning runtime hangs
# the corpus rather than failing it, and a gate that hangs is a gate somebody
# reruns. Two seconds is four orders of magnitude more than the unpinned path
# needs, so the number is not a race with anything.
fn parked(latch: CountDownLatch) -> String !io = {
  with handle Ask { ask(n) resume k => k(n + 1) }
  let n = ask(1)
  let ok = latch.await(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS!)
  "awaited=${to_string(ok)} n=${to_string(n)}"
}

pub fn main() -> Unit !io = {
  # One carrier for the whole program, set before anything touches
  # `VirtualThread` and therefore before its scheduler is built. With the
  # default parallelism a pinned carrier is simply one of four and `other`
  # runs on another one, which is how a pinning runtime would pass this file
  # without the two lines below.
  let _ = System.setProperty("jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.parallelism", "1")
  let _ = System.setProperty("jdk.virtualThreadScheduler.maxPoolSize", "1")

  let one = Thread.startVirtualThread(() => {
    io.println(installed_on_a_virtual_thread())
    io.println(suspended_across_a_sleep())
    io.println(yields_after_a_remount())
  })!
  one.join()

  let latch = CountDownLatch.new(1)
  let a = Thread.startVirtualThread(() => io.println(parked(latch)))!
  let b = Thread.startVirtualThread(() => {
    io.println("the other virtual thread got the carrier")
    latch.countDown()
  })!
  a.join()
  b.join()
}
